@@s001 Microbenchmark: from Mark0 to Mark8Setup
@@s002 Microbenchmark: from Mark0 to Mark8Setup
@@s003 How to measure how long a piece of Java code takes without being fooled by the JVM. The functions <code>Mark0</code>…<code>Mark8Setup</code> of exercise 8 are a ladder: each one fixes an error of the previous one.
@@s004 Where they come from: they are not standard
@@s005 The <code>Mark</code> functions <b>are not part of Java</b>: they were written by Peter Sestoft, one of the two lecturers. It shows in the header of <code>Benchmark.java</code> (<code>sestoft@itu.dk * 2013, 2015, 2026</code>) and in the reading indicated in the README of week 8: his note <i>Microbenchmarks in Java, C# and C</i>. <code>Mark0</code>…<code>Mark8</code> are the steps of that note. <code>Timer.java</code> describes itself as "crude wall clock timing utility" (a rough stopwatch).
@@s006 And outside the course?
@@s007 The tool used in practice for Java microbenchmarks is <b>JMH</b> (Java Microbenchmark Harness, from OpenJDK). It does professionally the same things as the <code>Mark</code> functions: warm-up, repetitions, mean and standard deviation. The course uses the <code>Mark</code> functions because they are short and can be read line by line. This information about JMH comes from general knowledge, not from the course files.
@@s008 Why a stopwatch is not enough
@@s009 The first idea: I take the time, run the code, take the time again and subtract. With Java it gives wrong numbers for three reasons.
@@s010 problem
@@s011 what happens
@@s012 how to avoid it
@@s013 <b>Warm-up</b>
@@s014 At first the code is <b>interpreted</b>, then the JIT compiler compiles it. The first executions are much slower.
@@s015 repeat until the time settles
@@s016 <b>Dead code</b>
@@s017 If the result is not used for anything, the JIT can remove the calculation and you measure nothing.
@@s018 use the result (<code>dummy += …</code>) and return it
@@s019 <b>Noise</b>
@@s020 Other processes, the garbage collector, the CPU frequency: every measurement is a bit different.
@@s021 repeat 10 times and give the <b>mean ± standard deviation</b>
@@s022 A real example, measured on this PC (JDK 26.0.2.1): <code>myPoint.hashCode()</code> in the first round costs <b>490 ns</b> (sdev 1164); after 134 million repetitions it costs <b>2.5 ns</b> (sdev 0.09). The code is identical: only the JVM has compiled it in the meantime.
@@s023 The ladder: each Mark fixes an error
@@s024 From Mark0 to Mark8Setup
@@s025 no:error,ok:fix,ora:what changes,dato:the measured data
@@s026 Useless: it includes the warm-up, and the precision of the stopwatch is not enough for a call of a few nanoseconds.
@@s027 The time is divided by 100 million, but the JIT can work out that <code>multiply(i)</code> is useless and remove it: you might be measuring an empty loop.
@@s028 Now the result is used (<code>dummy</code> comes out of the method): the JIT cannot eliminate the calculation.
@@s029 You can see the warm-up (the first lines are higher) and the variation between one repetition and the next.
@@s030 One number only, with its uncertainty. The formula is in the paragraph below.
@@s031 You do not have to guess how many calls are needed: you start from few and double, and stop when the round is long enough to be measured well. It prints one line for each doubling.
@@s032 You can measure any code: <code>Mark6('n', "hashCode()", i -&gt; myPoint.hashCode())</code>. It still prints one line for each doubling: it serves to see how it settles.
@@s033 This is the one to use to report a result: one line per measurement, without the warm-up lines.
@@s034 It serves to put a parameter in the line, for example the size of the array (<code>String.format("%8d", size)</code>).
@@s035 It prepares the data <b>without counting its time</b>: for example shuffling the array before each sort.
@@s036 Mark7 line by line
@@s037 Mark7 (from Benchmark.java)
@@s038 The method receives three things: the <b>time unit</b> (<code>'n'</code> nanoseconds, <code>'u'</code> microseconds, <code>'m'</code> milliseconds), a <b>message</b> to print, and the <b>function to measure</b> <code>f</code>. <code>LongToDoubleFunction</code> is a Java interface with a single method, <code>applyAsDouble(long)</code>: it takes a <code>long</code> and returns a <code>double</code>. That is why one passes a lambda <code>i -&gt; …</code>.
@@s039 <code>n</code> = how many repetitions of the same round are done, so that a mean can be computed. <code>count</code> = how many times <code>f</code> is called inside a round; it starts at 1 and is doubled immediately, so the first round has 2.
@@s040 <code>dummy</code> accumulates all the results of <code>f</code>: so the JIT cannot throw away the calculation. <code>st</code> = sum of the times, <code>sst</code> = sum of the <b>squares</b> of the times: they are needed for the standard deviation.
@@s041 Each round of the <code>do</code> doubles <code>count</code>: 2, 4, 8, … until the round lasts long enough.
@@s042 The sums are reset at each doubling: the mean concerns only the 10 repetitions of <b>this</b> <code>count</code>.
@@s043 A new stopwatch for each repetition. <code>new Timer()</code> starts it; <code>t.check()</code> returns the seconds elapsed.
@@s044 The measured work: it calls <code>f</code> <code>count</code> times, passing <code>i</code> (0, 1, 2, …). Passing <code>i</code> prevents the JIT from precomputing the result as a constant.
@@s045 Time of <b>one</b> call: seconds of the round × unit factor (<code>scale('n')</code> = 1e9) ÷ <code>count</code>.
@@s046 Updates the sums: <code>st</code> with the time, <code>sst</code> with its square. <code>totalCount</code> counts all the calls; it only serves for the returned value.
@@s047 Condition to <b>continue</b>: if the last repetition lasted less than 0.25 s, <code>count</code> is doubled and it is done again. When it lasts at least 0.25 s, the stopwatch is precise enough and one stops.
@@s048 Mean and standard deviation of the 10 repetitions (formula in the paragraph below).
@@s049 Prints a single line: message, mean, unit, sdev, <code>count</code> of the last round.
@@s050 Returns something that depends on all the results: the last way to make <code>dummy</code> be used.
@@s051 Mean and standard deviation
@@s052 The <b>standard deviation</b> (sdev) says how much the measurements are spread around the mean. If it is small compared with the mean, the measurement is stable; if it is large, it is not. The line <code>sdev = Math.sqrt((sst - mean*mean*n)/(n-1))</code> uses the two sums that the loop has already accumulated. An example with 4 times (the function uses 10):
@@s053 From 4 times to mean ± sdev
@@s054 dato:the times,due:the squares,ora:the formula,ok:the result
@@s055 Four repetitions of the same round.
@@s056 Sum of the times.
@@s057 Sum of the squares of the times.
@@s058 The mean.
@@s059 It divides by n−1 and not by n: this is the formula of the sample standard deviation.
@@s060 Check: the deviations from the mean are −1, 0, 0, +1, their squares sum to 2: the same thing, because Σ(t − mean)² = Σt² − n·mean². The advantage is that the loop does not have to remember the single times.
@@s061 How to read a line of Mark7
@@s062 <code>name mean unit sdev count</code>: for example <code>Thread virt cre strt join 14557,2 ns 864,61 32768</code> means that creating, starting and waiting for a virtual thread costs on average 14 557 ns, with sdev 865 ns (6% of the mean), measured over a round of 32 768 calls. An sdev close to or above the mean means the number is not reliable.
@@s063 Timer and Mark8Setup: measuring without the setup
@@s064 Timer.java and the heart of Mark8Setup
@@s065 <code>start</code> = the instant in which the stopwatch was (re)started; <code>spent</code> = the time already accumulated before a pause.
@@s066 <code>System.nanoTime()</code> is Java's clock in nanoseconds (useful only to subtract two instants). The measured time is <i>now − start + spent</i>, divided by 1e9 to have it in seconds.
@@s067 <code>pause()</code>: adds to <code>spent</code> the time elapsed since the last <code>start</code>. From then on, the time that passes <b>does not count</b> until <code>play()</code> is called again.
@@s068 <code>play()</code>: restarts from now. Everything that happened between <code>pause()</code> and <code>play()</code> stays out of <code>check()</code>.
@@s069 The stopwatch is stopped, the data is prepared (for example <code>shuffle</code> of the array, which costs 1.1 ms on 100 000 elements), it restarts, and only <code>f</code> is measured. Without this, the sort would also measure the shuffling.
@@s070 What exercises 8.1–8.4 measure
@@s071 The thread of the assignment: <b>how much a thing really costs</b> when in Java it seems free (a library function, a thread, a keyword)? Each exercise measures a different cost with the same <code>Mark</code> functions. The numbers are from this PC (i5-11400H, JDK 26.0.2.1).
@@s072 The assignment in 4 steps
@@s073 dato:what is measured,ok:result,ora:what one learns,no:warning
@@s074 <mark class="ora">One learns to use Mark7 and to read the sdev.</mark> In one run <code>tan</code> and <code>asin</code> had sdev ±5 ns, and in the other runs they were normal again: it was noise. <code>Math.sqrt</code> (≈2 ns) is a single CPU instruction; <code>Math.random</code> (≈20–29 ns) uses a shared <code>Random</code> with a CAS. And <code>sqrt(random)</code> is not <code>random + sqrt</code>: the 2 ns of <code>sqrt</code> disappear into the noise.
@@s075 <mark class="ora">Cost of the thread = total − work of the thread.</mark> The work (1000 increments) is worth ≈4.7 µs, so the overhead is ≈106 µs against ≈6.9 µs: about 15 times less for the virtual one. A platform thread should not be created for a small job. Careful: <mark class="no">"create + start" without join is not reliable</mark> (very high sdev).
@@s076 <mark class="ora">Parallelising pays off only if the work of one thread is worth more than the cost of the thread</mark> (≈7 µs for a virtual one, from the previous step). That is why the parallel quicksort creates a thread only if <code>j - a &gt; 50</code>: below that, sorting costs less than creating the thread.
@@s077 <mark class="ora">A <code>volatile</code> field costs about 20 times</mark> already with a single thread: every write has a memory barrier and the JIT cannot keep the field in a register. With two threads on the same object <code>addVolatile</code> reaches ≈31 ns per addition, while <code>add</code> does not change.
@@s078 The lesson that ties them together
@@s079 The cost is never "zero" and never "what it seems": a library call, a thread or a keyword can cost from a few ns to hundreds of µs. To decide (thread or not? <code>volatile</code> or not?) one must <b>measure rigorously</b>: warm-up, used result, repetitions, sdev. And that is why the <code>Mark</code> functions exist.
@@s080 Practical rules
@@s081 Things that falsify the measurement
@@s082 <b>Never inside an IDE or in debug mode</b> (the text of the exercise says so). Close the other applications; on a laptop connect the power. Always do at least two runs and look at the sdev: a much higher sdev in one run (as with <code>tan</code> at ±5.4 ns in one run out of two) indicates noise, not a property of the function. The value returned by <code>f</code> must <b>depend on the measured work</b>, otherwise the JIT eliminates it.
@@s083 Terminal commands
@@s084 From the project folder: <code>gradle -q -PmainClass=exercises08.Benchmark run</code> (change the class for the others: <code>TestTimeThreads</code>, <code>TestTimeSort</code>, <code>TestTimeVolatile</code>). <code>-q</code> prints only the output of the program; to save it add <code>&gt; file.txt</code>. Without Gradle: from the folder <code>app/src/main/java</code>, <code>javac exercises08/*.java</code> and then <code>java exercises08.Benchmark</code>.
